1	#!/usr/bin/env bun
2	/**
3	 * stop_on_pr_close.ts — closing a pull request an agent is working on asks that run to
4	 * stop, the way closing its issue already does.
5	 *
6	 * ## Why a pull request's close has to mean something
7	 *
8	 * A run was reviewing a pull request when the pull request merged. The merge reached
9	 * nothing: the run kept going for another nine minutes and stopped only when an unrelated
10	 * issue was closed, which reached it through the issue path. The structure was right and
11	 * the coverage was not — `issues: closed` stops a run, and `pull_request_target: closed`
12	 * did not, for either outcome (merged or closed unmerged).
13	 *
14	 * ## The mechanism is the existing one
15	 *
16	 * No second stop path. `STOP_TAG` in a comment, polled for by the running job, is the only
17	 * way anything outside a job reaches it — `/stop` and the issue close both work that way.
18	 * This is one more writer of the same tag.
19	 *
20	 * ## Why the gate is the claim, and not `runInFlight`
21	 *
22	 * Whether a run is in flight here is asked of the thread's open CLAIM
23	 * (`openClaimStopState`), not of `work-tree.ts`'s `runningOn`. That reader is right for
24	 * the tree and wrong for this: it is handed `merged`, and a merged pull request answers
25	 * "no run" by design (#449) — nothing can ever be started on one, so the "a request is
26	 * the newest word" half stops being evidence. That is exactly the node this has to reach:
27	 * the reviewer that merged its own pull request and is still watching its own thread. The
28	 * claim is written by a run that actually started, and a merge cannot invalidate it.
29	 *
30	 * ## Why a bot is not refused here, unlike an issue's close
31	 *
32	 * `stop_on_close.ts` no-ops a bot's close because an agent closing its own issue is how it
33	 * finishes, and stopping that run would cut it off mid-wrap-up. A pull request is the
34	 * other case: closing one is never how a run ends its own work. And there is nothing to
35	 * sift — a bot's merge or close raises no `pull_request_target` cascade at all (the header
36	 * of `atomaton-pr-closed.wac.ts` records that), so the event structure already answers
37	 * what the issue path's caller has to pass in.
38	 *
39	 * ## Why a stop that is already there is not repeated
40	 *
41	 * A close can reach the same run twice within a few seconds: this job, and an issue
42	 * close's subtree propagation, which posts its own tag on any node that is running. The
43	 * second write would be a second comment saying the same thing, so the thread is read
44	 * before it is written and a stop newer than the claim suppresses the write. A stop OLDER
45	 * than the claim is ignored by the watcher (it honours only what arrived after it started
46	 * watching) and so suppresses nothing.
47	 *
48	 * What that cannot cover is the propagation arriving AFTER this write — it has no gate of
49	 * its own. The cost is one duplicate comment; the watcher stops on the first one, so the
50	 * run behaves the same either way.
51	 *
52	 * Usage:
53	 *   stop_on_pr_close.ts --number N --merged true|false
54	 */
