430	 * The thread as it will read once the guard has finished with it.
431	 *
432	 * ## Why this exists
433	 *
434	 * The guard deletes a person's comment made while the ball is with an agent, and the
435	 * deletion is not instant: it is a `gh api DELETE` that may fail, and even when it
436	 * succeeds the comment is on the page until GitHub processes it. Every reader that
437	 * runs in that window — the guard's own next turn, the validation that resolves who a
438	 * pull request asks for, the dispatch that checks for a request already outstanding —
439	 * would otherwise see a comment that is on its way out and read it as though it were
440	 * staying.
441	 *
442	 * So the shaping is one function, and every reader uses it. A comment the guard would
443	 * remove is removed here too, so no two readers disagree about what the thread says.
444	 *
445	 * ## What it removes
446	 *
447	 * A person's comment that arrived while the ball was already with an agent — the
448	 * guard's rule, applied to the whole thread in order: walk it oldest first, and drop
449	 * each such comment as it is reached. It is any human comment, not only a command: a
450	 * person's remark made mid-run would otherwise sit unseen until the run finished, and
451	 * the guard removes it for the same reason it removes a command.
452	 *
453	 * A comment that arrives while the ball is with a person is kept — it is the request
